 "caption": "These are fractions of PICKED objects, not molar ratios: each stream's picker and 2D window biases what it sees (rods: ellipse blob 20\u2013180 \u00c5 + top-50 % local power; anchors: 30\u201350 \u00c5 circle blob + top-50 % local power, random 25 % of micrographs). Bars: pooled particle-weighted fraction, with value and 95 % percentile bootstrap CI over micrographs (2,000 resamples); grey dots: the 20 grid squares. Bottom: per-square fractions with within-square bootstrap CIs. Q/df = between-square scatter relative to within-square sampling error (chi-square, 19 df); every category varies between squares far beyond sampling (Q/df 14\u2013221), so the pooled CI understates square-to-square variation. The variation tracks ice and crowding across squares (Spearman over squares), and the two are confounded: square median relative ice spans only 1.06\u20131.09 and falls as crowding rises (\u03c1 ice vs detected dots per micrograph -0.91), so their effects cannot be separated. Anchor 'two dots' rises with detected dots per micrograph (\u03c1 +0.95; independent of the 2D labels) and with rod-stream particles per micrograph (\u03c1 +0.80), and falls with median ice (\u03c1 -0.87); free rods rise with ice (\u03c1 +0.84; vs detected dots \u03c1 -0.86). (Anchors per micrograph, \u03c1 +0.94, is not used: it includes the dot-pair category itself.) Rod-stream particles in unreviewed (<270-particle) classes (5,654) are excluded.",
 "figure": "figures/final/census.png",
 "tables": [
  "results/final/census.csv",
  "results/final/census_by_square.csv"
 ],
